          "answer_excerpt": "ALPHA code is MAPLE-902657.\nBETA code is RIVER-435643.\nGAMMA code is CLOUD-912485.\n\n# High-Performance LLM Inference: A Technical Deep Dive into Continuous Batching, KV Caches, and Advanced Decoding Strategies\n\nThe deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in production environments presents a unique set of engineering challenges that differ significantly from traditional machine learning inference tasks. Unlike classification or regression models, which typically process fixed-size inputs and produce fixed-size outputs in a single forward pass, LLMs are autoregressive. They generate text token-by-token, where each new token depends on the entire sequence of previously generated tokens. This sequential dependency creates a fundamental tension between latency (the time it takes to generate the first token and subsequent tokens) and throughput (the number of tokens or requests processed per second). To resolve this tension, modern LLM inference servers have evolved sophisticated mechanisms including continuous batching, Key-Value (KV) caching, prefix reuse, and speculative decoding. This tutorial provides a detailed technical analysis of these mechanisms, explaining how they interact to optimize performance in high-concurrency environments.\n\n## 1. The Fundamental Bottleneck: Memory Bandwidth vs. Compute\n\nTo understand why these optimizations are necessary, one must first understand the hardware bottleneck in LLM inference. During the prefill phase (processing the input prompt), the model is compute-bound. It processes the entire input sequence in parallel, utilizing the massive parallel processing power of GPUs (Tensor Cores) to perform matrix multiplications. However, during the decode phase (generating output tokens), the model becomes memory-bandwidth-bound.\n\nIn the decode phase, the model generates one token at a time. For each token, the model must read the entire model weights from High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) to the GPU\